Create an autonomous Codex implementation prompt from the supplied milestone or roadmap document.

Purpose:

Convert the supplied roadmap/milestone/backlog document into a complete Codex-ready implementation prompt using the same Project443-DigitalTwin delivery pattern:

- one milestone at a time
- preserve security controls
- run focused tests
- run full test suite
- commit and push after each milestone
- continue automatically unless a defined stop condition is met
- include mandatory security review gates where appropriate
- include exact milestone-specific requirements from the supplied document

Input document:

Use the supplied milestone or roadmap document as the source of truth.

Do not invent milestones that are not in the document.

Do not omit milestones from the document.

Do not water down security, testing, documentation, or acceptance criteria.

Ask no follow-up questions unless the document is too ambiguous to produce a safe implementation prompt.

Output required:

Produce one complete copy/paste-ready prompt for Codex.

The generated prompt must include the following sections:

1. Title / opening instruction

   Example:

   Continue Project443-DigitalTwin <roadmap theme> milestone delivery autonomously.

2. Working directory

   Use this unless the input document specifies otherwise:

   C:\dev\Project443-DigitalTwin\digital-twin-starter

3. Primary objective

   Summarise what the milestone set is intended to implement.

4. Source of truth

   Name the supplied roadmap/milestone document.

5. Scope boundary

   Include:

   This is Project443-DigitalTwin work.

   Do not make changes in:

   C:\dev\Project443-DigitalTwin\Transcripty

   Do all work only in:

   C:\dev\Project443-DigitalTwin\digital-twin-starter

6. Milestone order

   Extract every milestone from the document in order.

   Include:

   - milestone number
   - milestone title
   - backlog/source ID if present

7. Continuation behaviour

   Include strong instructions that Codex must not stop after one, two, or three milestones.

   Include:

   A completion summary is not the end of the task. A completion summary is only a checkpoint before immediately starting the next milestone.

   A security review summary is not the end of the task. A security review summary is only a checkpoint before immediately starting the next milestone, provided continuation is approved.

8. Stop conditions

   Include:

   - all milestones complete
   - full test suite failure cannot be fixed safely
   - requirement missing or ambiguous enough that implementation would be guesswork
   - git push fails due to remote conflicts or authentication
   - major architecture decision required outside the roadmap
   - proposed implementation would weaken completed security controls
   - security review identifies a Critical or High finding that cannot be fixed safely without clarification

9. Non-stop conditions

   Include:

   Do not stop merely because:

   - one milestone is finished
   - two or three milestones are finished
   - five milestones are finished
   - a security review was completed
   - a full test suite passed
   - a commit was created
   - a push succeeded
   - a completion note was written
   - the next milestone was selected

   Those are continue conditions, not stop conditions.

10. Pre-flight instructions

   Include:

   - git status
   - confirm working directory
   - confirm remote
   - read the supplied roadmap
   - identify last completed milestone
   - check whether previous committed milestone had a passing full test suite
   - run baseline only if needed

11. No repeated baseline rule

   Include the existing rule:

   Do not run another full baseline `python -m pytest` before starting the next milestone if the previous milestone has just completed with a passing full `python -m pytest`.

12. Operating loop

   Include a numbered loop covering:

   - identify current milestone
   - read full milestone definition
   - implement only that milestone
   - preserve traceability to backlog/source ID
   - add/update relevant code, schemas, services, migrations, APIs, UI links, scripts, config, docs, tests
   - preserve security controls
   - run focused tests
   - fix and retest
   - run full suite once
   - update docs
   - commit and push
   - record checkpoint note
   - continue to next milestone or security review gate

13. Security controls

   Include baseline Project443 controls:

   - explicit auth on every new non-health route
   - mutating routes reject viewer-only users
   - admin-only routes reject viewer/operator users
   - workspace-scoped data enforces workspace_id
   - do not expose or persist secrets, tokens, API keys, webhook URLs, raw payloads, filesystem paths, connector secrets, model internals, note content, or sensitive operational internals
   - startup/config validation fails safely
   - connector and provider health payloads are redacted
   - production-capable settings must not fail open
   - AI review, confidence, provenance, and writeback controls remain intact

   Add any extra security controls from the supplied document.

14. Focused test guidance

   Extract specific test expectations from the supplied document.

   Also include generic expectations:

   - regression test for the backlog finding
   - happy path
   - validation failures
   - unauthorized/wrong-role/allowed-role
   - workspace isolation
   - migration compatibility
   - fresh database bootstrap if relevant
   - redaction
   - error handling
   - backwards compatibility

15. Security review gates

   If the document specifies review gates, reproduce them exactly.

   If the document does not specify review gates, add:

   Perform a mandatory security review after every 5 completed milestones and at the final milestone.

   Security review output path:

   docs/security/security_review_after_milestone_<N>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.md

   Include the full standard security review structure:

   - Scope
   - Standards Reference
   - Executive Summary
   - Findings
   - Regression Review
   - Route and Operation Security Matrix
   - Workspace Isolation Review
   - Connector / Provider / AI Review where applicable
   - Supply Chain / Deployment Review
   - Test Evidence
   - Recommendations
   - Assumptions

16. Milestone-specific requirements

   For each milestone in the document, include a subsection:

   Milestone <N> - <Title>

   Backlog/source ID: <ID if present>

   Implement and verify:

   - all deliverables from the document

   Acceptance criteria:

   - all acceptance criteria from the document

   Security controls:

   - all milestone-specific controls from the document

   Dependencies or out-of-scope items:

   - include if present

17. General rules

   Include:

   - work strictly one milestone at a time
   - do not batch multiple milestones into one commit
   - do not batch a security review into a feature milestone commit
   - do not skip focused tests
   - do not skip full suite
   - do not rewrite old tests to hide broken implementation
   - keep repo green and pushed after every milestone
   - include git commit/push command after every build milestone response

18. Specific first task

   Start with the first incomplete milestone in the supplied document.

   Include that milestone’s deliverables explicitly.

19. Begin now

   End with:

   Begin now with Milestone <N>.

